[Bird](https://bird.com) is a communications platform with APIs for sending email and SMS. For email, you verify a sending domain in your Bird workspace, create an API key, and send through Bird's HTTP email API or its SMTP relay.

# How does the integration work?

You can use the Bird email provider in Appwrite Messaging to send customized emails to your users, such as reminders, promotions, announcements, and custom authentication flows. Emails can be sent immediately or scheduled, to [topics](/docs/products/messaging/topics), users, or individual [targets](/docs/products/messaging/targets).

The provider talks to Bird's HTTP email API, so it needs a Bird API key rather than SMTP settings. Appwrite reads the region from the key's `bk_<region>_` prefix and sends each recipient a message of their own, with Bird's `transactional` category. On Appwrite Cloud, one-click setup connects your Bird workspace and creates that key for you, with only the `emails:write` scope.

The Bird email provider is live on Appwrite Cloud and ships in the next self-hosted Appwrite release. To send SMS through Bird as well, see [SMS with Bird](/integrations/sms-bird).

{% info title="One-click setup is available on Appwrite Cloud" %}
One-click setup relies on Bird being configured as an OAuth2 provider for the Appwrite Cloud Console, so it only appears on Appwrite Cloud. On self-hosted Appwrite, create the API key in Bird and enter it by hand.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To implement the Bird email provider in Appwrite Messaging, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Verify a sending domain in Bird

First, sign up for [Bird](https://bird.com) and open your workspace. Under **Email** > **Domains**, add the domain you want to send from. Bird recommends a subdomain such as `mail.example.com`. Add the DKIM, return-path, and DMARC records Bird shows you to your DNS, and wait for the domain to verify. Bird only sends from verified domains. [Bird's sending domains guide](https://bird.com/docs/guides/email/sending-domains) covers each record.

## Step 2: Add the Bird provider to your Appwrite project

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already. In your Appwrite project, head to **Messaging** > **Providers** and click **Add provider**. Bird is listed under both **Email** and **SMS**. Choose **Bird** under **Email**.

![Configure Bird step for the email provider, with the One-click setup card above the API key and sender fields](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/configure-bird-email.avif)

In the **Configure Bird** step, use one-click setup or fill in the fields by hand.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #one-click title="One-click setup" %}
On Appwrite Cloud, the **Configure Bird** step opens with a **One-click setup** card above the form.

1. Click **Connect with Bird** and authorize Appwrite in Bird.
2. Select a verified **Domain**. If none of your domains are verified yet, click **Manage domains**, verify one in Bird, and click **Check again**.
3. Click **Generate API key**.
4. Review the filled-in fields and click **Create provider**.

![One-click setup card after connecting Bird, with a verified domain selected and the Generate API key button](/images/docs/messaging/providers/bird/one-click-bird-email.avif)

Appwrite creates an API key named `Appwrite SMTP: <project name>` in your Bird workspace, with only the `emails:write` scope. It also fills any empty fields: the sender email becomes `noreply@<domain>` unless you already typed an address on that domain, the sender name defaults to your project name, and the provider name defaults to `One-click Bird sender`. If you leave the wizard without creating the provider, Appwrite revokes the key it generated.
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #manual title="Configure manually" %}
In the Bird dashboard, go to **Platform tools** > **API keys** and create a key with the `emails` scope at the `write` level. Copy it when it's shown, because Bird only displays the full key once. Then fill in the following fields in Appwrite:

| Field name | |
| --- | --- |
| Name | A name for the provider in Appwrite. |
| API key | The API key you created in Bird. It starts with `bk_` and your region, for example `bk_eu1_`. |
| Sender name | The sender name that appears in the emails sent from this provider. |
| Sender email | The provider sends emails from this address. It must be on a domain verified in Bird. |

Click **Create provider**. The provider is enabled once it has both an API key and a sender email.
{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

## Step 3: Test the Bird provider

Before you proceed, you must already have at least one [topic](/docs/products/messaging/topics) or [target](/docs/products/messaging/targets) set up.

Go to **Messaging** > **Messages**, click **Create message**, and choose **Email**. Write a test message, select one of your test targets, set the schedule to **Now**, and click **Send**. Check that the email arrives in your inbox. If it doesn't, check the logs in the Appwrite Console and in your Bird workspace.

Bird refuses placeholder domains such as `example.com` and `test.com`, so give your test users real addresses. To test without a real inbox, use one of [Bird's sandbox addresses](https://bird.com/docs/guides/email/testing-sandbox), such as `delivered@messagebird.dev`, as an email target.

To send emails from your backend instead, follow the [Send email messages](/docs/products/messaging/send-email-messages) guide with an [Appwrite Server SDK](/docs/sdks#server).

